'''''Adiantum concinnum''''', locally known as '''''cilantrillo''''',  '''''culantrillo''''', or the '''''maidenhair fern''''' is a [[Rhizome|rhizomatous]] [[wiktionary:geophyte|geophyte]] used as a local [[herbal medicine]] in many [[South America|South]] and [[Central America|Central American]] cultures.

== Description ==
''Adiantum concinnum'' is a rhizomatous geophyte of the genus ''[[Adiantum]]''. It can have either an erect or suberect [[rhizome]], its [[frond]]s having [[Pinna (botany)|pinnae]] that are ovate or oblong-deltoid and {{cvt|3|-|4|cm}} by {{cvt|1.5|-|2|cm|1}}. Each pinnule possesses up to 10 pairs of pinnae. The fronds of these plants are either pale green or dark green and are fan shaped. The [[Sorus|sori]] are distributed all along the pinnae.<ref name=powo>{{BioRef|powo |id=315077-2 |title=''Adiantum concinnum'' Humb. & Bonpl. ex Willd.}}</ref><ref name="for">{{cite web |url=https://indiabiodiversity.org/species/show/267146#demography-and-conservation |title= Adiantum concinnum Humb. & Bonpl. ex Willd. |last=Rajesh |first=K.P. |date=<!--Not Stated--> |website= India Biodiversity Portal |publisher= Technology Partner Strand Life Sciences}}</ref>

== Habitat ==
''Adiantum concinnum'' is native to a wide geographical area ranging from [[Northern Mexico]] to eastern parts of [[Brazil]]. It has since been introduced to parts of [[India]], [[Sri Lanka]], [[Hawaii]], and [[Tanzania]]. The ferns enjoy a humid tropical biome,<ref name="for"/> and have been observed in altitudes lower than {{cvt|1,500|m}}.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Adiantum concinnum Humb. & Bonpl. ex Willd., Polished maidenhair (World flora) |url=https://identify.plantnet.org/k-world-flora/species/Adiantum%20concinnum%20Humb.%20&%20Bonpl.%20ex%20Willd./data |access-date=2024-04-21 |website=Pl@ntNet |language=en}}</ref>

==Cultivation==
''Adiantum concinnum'' can be grown in moist [[potting soil]]. It prefers lower levels of light, and does not tolerate cool temperatures. A cultivar, 'Edwinii', is easier to grow and less sensitive to cold; it may, in fact, be a hybrid with ''[[Adiantum raddianum]]''.<ref>{{cite book | first1=Barbara Joe | last1=Hoshizaki | first2=Robbin C. | last2=Moran | title=Fern Grower's Manual | year=2001 | publisher=Timber Press | location=Portland, Oregon | isbn=9780881924954 |pages=162–163}}</ref>

